## Pull request overview

Copilot reviewed 13 out of 13 changed files in this pull request and generated no new comments.




<details>
<summary>Suppressed comments (2)</summary>

**src/ring_race.zig:395**
* The tests for `valid()` currently assert that a window of only padding (`{0}`) is valid. If `valid()` rejects all-padding windows (so `validated` can’t be incremented without observing at least one writer-produced sample), the tests should be updated accordingly to keep the harness’s overlap signal meaningful.
```
test "a window of consecutive samples behind a zero pad is valid" {
    try testing.expect(valid(&[_]f32{ 0, 0, 1, 2, 3 }));
    try testing.expect(valid(&[_]f32{ 7, 8, 9 }));
    try testing.expect(valid(&[_]f32{0}));
    try testing.expect(valid(&[_]f32{}));
```
**src/ring_race.zig:378**
* `valid()` currently returns true for a window that is entirely padding (all zeros). That makes `Counters.validated`/the overlap check weaker than the surrounding comments imply, because a reader could "validate" a window that carried no sample the writer produced (e.g. if the written() gate regressed or indexing broke). Treat an all-zero window as invalid so "validated" always implies at least one non-zero sample was observed.

This issue also appears on line 391 of the same file.
```
fn valid(window: []const f32) bool {
    var index: usize = 0;
    while (index < window.len and window[index] == 0) index += 1;

    while (index + 1 < window.len) : (index += 1) {
```
</details>
